Pr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" lvl="0" marL="0" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2000"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2000" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>generate xylulose-5-phosphate for </a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" lvl="0" marL="0" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2000"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2000" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>nonoxidative PPP.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="An image depicting the oxidative reactions of the pentose phosphate pathway. At the top is glucose 6-phosphate.&#xA;This begins with HCOH in a blue box, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HC. The HCOH in a blue box also bonds up, then right and down to O, which bonds down, then left to HC. HC then bonds down to CH2OPO3 with a minus 2 charge on it. Below this is an arrow pointing down to 6-phospho-glucano-[gamma]-lactone. To the left of the arrow is glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase. To the right of the arrow is a curved arrow pointing from NADP+ down to NADPH (in a pink box) [plus] H+. In the arc is Mg2+. 6-phospho-glucono-[gamma]-lactone begins with C, which double bonds right to O in a blue box. This bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HC. C in the blue box also bonds up, then right, then down to O, which bonds down, then left to HC. HC then bonds down to CH2OPO3 with a minus 2 charge on it. Below this is a down arrow pointing to 6-phosphogluconate. To the left of the arrow is lactonase in blue. On the right, H20 bonds to this arrow. Mg2+ is to the right of the arrow. 6-phosphogluconate begins with C, which double bonds up and left to O and bonds up and right to O-. This is all in a blue box. C also bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to CH2OPO3 with a minus 2 charge on it. Below this is a down arrow pointing to D-ribulose 5-phosphate. To the left of the arrow is 6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase in blue. To the right is a curved arrow pointing from NADP+ down to NADPH (in a red box) [plus] H+. In the arc is Mg2+. There is also an arrow jutting right to CO2 in blue. D-ribulose 5-phosphate begins with C, which bonds up to CH2OH, double bonds right to O, and bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to CH2OPO3 with a minus 2 charge on it. Below this are reversible arrows. To the left of the arrows is phosphopentose isomerase in blue. Below this is D-ribose 5-phosphate in a pink box, where CHO bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to HCOH, which bonds down to CH2OPO3 with a minus 2 charge on it." id="684" name="Google Shape;684;p93"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="6340" l="49353" r="0" t="44230"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="2316162" y="2014537"/><a:ext cx="2484437" cy="2690812"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr id="685" name="Google Shape;685;p93"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId4"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="1752600" y="4533900"/><a:ext cx="1524000" cy="344487"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="An image showing the nonoxidative phase, where ribulose 5-phosphate is first epimerized to xylulose 5-phosphate. On the left is ribulose 5-phosphate. It begins with C bonding up to CH2OH, double bonding right to O and bonding down to C, which bonds left to H, right to OH, and down to C, which bonds left to H, right to OH, and down to CH2OPO2 over 3[negative]. To the right are reversible arrows. Below these is ribulose 5-phosphate epimerase in blue. To the right is xylulose 5-phosphate, where C bonds up to CH2OH, double bonds right to O, and bonds down to C, which bonds left to HO, right to H, and down to C, which bonds left to H, right to OH, and down to CH2OPO3 with a minus 2 charge on it." id="686" name="Google Shape;686;p93"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId5"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="4953000" y="4533900"/><a:ext cx="3633787" cy="2211387"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>